<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indira Gandhi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1984">1984</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indira <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> (1917–1984) was an Indian politician, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a>, who served as prime minister from 1966 to 1977 and from 1980 until her death, the only woman to hold the office. She nationalised the banks, led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in the war of 1971 and ruled by decree during the Emergency of 1975–1977. She was assassinated by her <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> bodyguards after ordering the army into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Congress governed almost without interruption until 1977, under Nehru, Lal Bahadur Shastri and <b>Indira Gandhi</b>, who nationalised the banks in 1969.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In October Prime Minister <b>Indira Gandhi</b> was assassinated by two of her Sikh bodyguards, and thousands of Sikhs were killed in the violence that followed in Delhi and other cities.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Bandung also brought together U Nu, V. K. Krishna Menon, Ho Chi Minh, Zhou Enlai, Norodom Sihanouk, U Thant and a young <b>Indira Gandhi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance to <b>Indira Gandhi</b>’s Emergency of 1975 to 1977 brought rapid growth, Jan Sangh leaders such as Atal Bihari Vajpayee and L. K. Advani rose to national prominence, and the Bharatiya Janata Party was founded in April 1980.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1984 Rakesh Sharma, the first Indian astronaut, used its opening line to describe to Prime Minister <b>Indira Gandhi</b> how India looked from space, and Manmohan Singh quoted the poem at his first press conference as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress parliamentary party elected <b>Indira Gandhi</b> as its leader after the sudden death of Lal Bahadur Shastri.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
